## The story, as data: who's in it, what they say, and the order things happen in.
##
## A scene is a set (a background drawn by code) and a list of lines:
##   [speaker, text]               a line of dialogue ("*" is narration, no portrait)
##   ["choice", [[label, flag], ...]]   Leo picks; the flag is remembered
##   ["set", name]                 go somewhere else mid-scene (a fade through black)
##   ["cast", [[who, x, facing, pose], ...]]   who's standing there now (after a "set")
##   ["cash", amount]              money changes hands
##   ["flag", name]                remember something
##   ["debt", amount]              what Leo owes the Familia changes (Mia's pencil; never below zero)
##   ["pose", who, pose]           someone in the scene changes pose (stand, crossed, pockets, cup, phone, bow, point, hips, wave)
## Any line (or a choice's option) can end with a condition, {"karma": "low"}, {"flag": "kid_home"},
## {"not_flag": "x"}: it only plays for a Leo it fits (see Karma.holds). People talk to a reckless
## Leo differently from a calm one, and the endings branch the same way (a step's "if").
## A scene's "cast" is who's standing in the set: [who, x (0..320), facing (1 right / -1 left), pose].
## Anyone who speaks but isn't in the cast is on Leo's phone (texts).
## Text can use {MANAGER} (the old manager's name), {CASH} and {DEBT}.
class_name StoryScript
extends RefCounted

